<!-- CARD GRID -->
<main class="grid">

  <!-- TEXT 1 -->
  <article class="card" id="text1">
    <div class="card-number">01</div>
    <div class="card-accent-bar" style="background:#c84b2f"></div>
    <h2 class="card-title">What if the internet collapsed?</h2>
    <p class="card-source">Al Nebi – Linguapress · Informative / speculative</p>
    <p class="card-summary">
      A speculative article asking what would happen if the entire internet failed worldwide. It opens with relatable moments of local outage before scaling up to a global scenario. Optimists believe the internet is too resilient to collapse globally; but a growing number of engineers warn it is not impossible. If it did fail: financial systems crash, supply chains break, hospitals fall into chaos, governments declare emergencies. Over time, societies adapt — returning to 1990s-style communication — while gaining unexpected benefits like less cybercrime and lower energy use. The article ends on a forward-looking note about resilience and local networks.
    </p>
    <div class="themes">
      <span class="theme-tag">Technology & fragility</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Global interdependence</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Resilience</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Optimism vs pessimism</span>
    </div>

    <div class="context-section">
      <h4>Rhetorical structure</h4>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-thesis">Thesis</span><span>The internet is fragile and a global collapse, while unlikely, is not impossible.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-counter">Counter</span><span>Optimists: the internet is too decentralised to fail at global scale.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-evidence">Evidence</span><span>Past local outages (Spain, USA) give a glimpse of what global failure would look like.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-concede">Silver lining</span><span>Lower energy use, no cybercrime, less social media pressure.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-conclude">Conclusion</span><span>World reorganises around local networks and physical systems — a new technological era.</span></div>
    </div>

    <button class="vocab-toggle" onclick="toggleVocab(this)">▶ Show key vocabulary (6 words)</button>
    <div class="vocab-panel">
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">fragile</span><span class="vocab-def">easily broken; vulnerable to failure</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">resilient</span><span class="vocab-def">able to recover quickly from difficulties</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">uncentralized</span><span class="vocab-def">spread out; not controlled from one point</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">grind to a halt</span><span class="vocab-def">stop completely, slowly and painfully</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">supply chain</span><span class="vocab-def">the network moving goods from producer to consumer</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">misinformation</span><span class="vocab-def">false or inaccurate information spread widely</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grammar-box show">
      <strong>Grammar note:</strong> Future simple ('will fail', 'will grind to a halt') is used throughout the consequences section — this creates a tone of certainty about predicted outcomes. Also: 'too [adj] to [verb]' = so much of a quality that the action is impossible (e.g. "too resilient to fail").
    </div>

    <div class="card-footer">
      <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="downloadSummary(1)">⬇ Download summary</button>
      <button class="btn btn-secondary" onclick="toggleVocab(this.closest('.card').querySelector('.vocab-toggle'))">Toggle vocab</button>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- TEXT 2 -->
  <article class="card" id="text2">
    <div class="card-number">02</div>
    <div class="card-accent-bar" style="background:#2f6b8f"></div>
    <h2 class="card-title">Aeroponics: feeding tomorrow's world?</h2>
    <p class="card-source">Linguapress · Informative / argumentative</p>
    <p class="card-summary">
      A scientific-argumentative article making the case for aeroponics — growing plants with roots suspended in air, misted with a nutrient-rich solution — as a sustainable food technology. It begins with the global food waste problem (UN FAO: ~50% of fruit and vegetables wasted) and the pressures of population growth. It then compares aeroponics to traditional and hydroponic farming, showing aeroponics eliminates water waste entirely, avoids nutrient run-off (unlike conventional farms in Brittany, France), and can be practised anywhere — including indoors. Chicago's O'Hare airport has hosted an aeroponic garden since 2011. The article concludes that local aeroponic production could dramatically cut the environmental cost of shipping food thousands of miles.
    </p>
    <div class="themes">
      <span class="theme-tag">Food sustainability</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Innovation vs tradition</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Resource waste</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Population pressure</span>
    </div>

    <div class="context-section">
      <h4>Rhetorical structure</h4>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-thesis">Thesis</span><span>Aeroponics reduces natural resource use and waste — essential for a sustainable future.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-evidence">Evidence</span><span>UN FAO: 50% of fruit/veg wasted. Chicago vegetables travel 3,000+ km. NASA: Southern California 4.2 gigatons water deficit/year.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-concede">Concession</span><span>Eliminating all waste is impossible — but reducing from 50% toward 0% is realistic.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-conclude">Conclusion</span><span>Local aeroponic farms in cities = huge cut in water, minerals, transport costs at global scale.</span></div>
    </div>

    <button class="vocab-toggle" onclick="toggleVocab(this)">▶ Show key vocabulary (7 words)</button>
    <div class="vocab-panel">
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">aeroponics</span><span class="vocab-def">growing plants in air/mist, roots sprayed with nutrients — no soil or water trough</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">hydroponics</span><span class="vocab-def">growing plants in nutrient-rich water (step before aeroponics)</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">wastage</span><span class="vocab-def">the amount wasted; unnecessary loss of a resource</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">nutrient</span><span class="vocab-def">a substance that plants/animals need to grow and stay healthy</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">algal bloom</span><span class="vocab-def">rapid algae growth caused by excess nutrients — harms water ecosystems</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">self-sufficient</span><span class="vocab-def">able to produce everything one needs without importing</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">optimum</span><span class="vocab-def">the best or most favourable conditions</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grammar-box show">
      <strong>Grammar note:</strong> Gerund as subject — "Cutting out all waste … is an impossible goal." The gerund (-ing form) acts as the subject of the sentence. Also watch for conditional urgency: "If our world is to survive … we have no choice but to…"
    </div>

    <div class="card-footer">
      <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="downloadSummary(2)">⬇ Download summary</button>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- TEXT 3 -->
  <article class="card" id="text3">
    <div class="card-number">03</div>
    <div class="card-accent-bar" style="background:#4a7c3f"></div>
    <h2 class="card-title">William Fox Talbot & the origins of photography</h2>
    <p class="card-source">Linguapress · Biographical / historical</p>
    <p class="card-summary">
      A biographical article tracing the invention of photography to William Henry Fox Talbot (1800–), an English polymath elected to the Royal Society at 31. While travelling Europe in the 1830s and using a camera obscura to sketch, Fox Talbot began experimenting with light-sensitive chemicals on paper to fix images automatically — producing the 'calotype'. In 1839, French inventor Louis Daguerre announced a rival process (the daguerrotype, using metal plates). Fox Talbot immediately responded with his own announcement. The calotype's negative/positive paper process proved simpler and became the international standard. Within 40 years others improved it; photography spread across Europe and North America. The article ends by contrasting Fox Talbot's slow process with today's instant digital photography.
    </p>
    <div class="themes">
      <span class="theme-tag">History of invention</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Science & discovery</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Competition & progress</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Then vs now</span>
    </div>

    <div class="context-section">
      <h4>Rhetorical structure</h4>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-personal">Personal history</span><span>Fox Talbot: born 1800, Cambridge, elected Royal Society at 31. Broad education: classics, chemistry, Hebrew, botany, maths.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-evidence">Key fact</span><span>The oldest surviving photo in the world: the 'Oriel window' at Lacock, 1835.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-counter">Rival</span><span>Daguerre's daguerrotype (1839) — more complex, metal plates, positive image.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-conclude">Outcome</span><span>Calotype (negative/positive on paper) won out and became the world standard.</span></div>
    </div>

    <button class="vocab-toggle" onclick="toggleVocab(this)">▶ Show key vocabulary (6 words)</button>
    <div class="vocab-panel">
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">calotype</span><span class="vocab-def">Fox Talbot's photographic process — negative image on chemically treated paper</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">daguerrotype</span><span class="vocab-def">Daguerre's rival process — positive image on metal plate, more complex</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">impregnated</span><span class="vocab-def">soaked with / infused with (chemicals)</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">camera obscura</span><span class="vocab-def">a box with a lens that projects an external image inside — used for drawing</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">the latter</span><span class="vocab-def">the second of two things just mentioned (formal linking device)</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">contemporaries</span><span class="vocab-def">people living or working at the same time as the person mentioned</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grammar-box show">
      <strong>Grammar note:</strong> "While Daguerre's process produced a positive image on metal, Fox Talbot's produced a negative image on paper" — 'while' introduces a contrast clause. Also: "the latter finally disappeared" — 'the latter' always refers to the second of two previously named items.
    </div>

    <div class="card-footer">
      <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="downloadSummary(3)">⬇ Download summary</button>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- TEXT 4a -->
  <article class="card" id="text4a">
    <div class="card-number">4a</div>
    <div class="card-accent-bar" style="background:#7a4fc4"></div>
    <h2 class="card-title">Dorothea Lange: a visionary whose camera never lied</h2>
    <p class="card-source">Abridged from The Guardian · Profile / literary journalism</p>
    <p class="card-summary">
      A vivid profile of American documentary photographer Dorothea Lange (1895–1965). In 1936, driving through California, Lange photographed a destitute pea-pickers camp and captured the 'Migrant Mother' — a 32-year-old woman from Oklahoma with 6 children. Published in the San Francisco News, it prompted the federal government to rush aid. Hired by the Farm Security Administration, Lange documented Depression-era agricultural workers. Her courage showed early: she took her mother's maiden name when her father deserted the family, studied photography before she could afford a camera, and contracted polio leaving her with a limp she said "formed me, guided me, instructed me". The text praises her empathy, literary titling of images, and collaboration with John Steinbeck. Her approach: "A camera is a tool for learning how to see without a camera."
    </p>
    <div class="themes">
      <span class="theme-tag">Art with social purpose</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Justice & inequality</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Individual vs situation</span>
      <span class="theme-tag">Courage & adversity</span>
    </div>

    <div class="context-section">
      <h4>Rhetorical structure</h4>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-thesis">Argument</span><span>Art has real uses — Lange's photography is the proof; it changed government policy.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-personal">Personal history</span><span>Polio, absent father, self-taught; her adversity shaped her empathy.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-evidence">Impact</span><span>Federal aid to the camp within days of publication. Hired by Farm Security Administration.</span></div>
      <div class="context-item"><span class="ctx-label ctx-quote">Key quote</span><span>"I never steal a photograph." / "A camera is a tool for learning how to see without a camera."</span></div>
    </div>

    <button class="vocab-toggle" onclick="toggleVocab(this)">▶ Show key vocabulary (6 words)</button>
    <div class="vocab-panel">
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">visionary</span><span class="vocab-def">someone with original ideas; here = one who saw truth others missed</span></div>
      <div class="vocab-entry"><span class="vocab-word">destitute</span><span class="vocab-def">extremely poor; without basic necessities</span></div>
